---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('klaviyo')!;
const b = appBySlug('postscript')!;
const path = '/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can Klaviyo email and Postscript SMS work together?",
    "answer": "Yes, but prove channel ownership, purchase exits and offer consistency in the actual integration before enabling both."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Postscript only offer SMS?",
    "answer": "No. Its public site also advertises Email; the pricing page currently links to an email waitlist, so confirm account access before planning an email migration."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does coupon sync include all offer terms?",
    "answer": "No. Postscript says expiration and discount details do not pass with the latest coupon code; maintain and verify those terms separately."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are the basket examples recovery results?",
    "answer": "No. They are hypothetical fixed-goods-cost calculations and exclude messaging and other operating expenses."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Assign email and SMS ownership before paying for a second recovery system.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{label: 'Keep or improve', tool: a.name, reason: 'Price the required repair in the existing recovery workflow.'}, {label: 'Evaluate', tool: b.name, reason: "Coordinate the SMS branch and shared coupon handoff."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Choose an owner for each channel</h2>
<p>A store with working Klaviyo email should first compare keeping recovery in that account with assigning SMS to Postscript. The additional system must earn its integration and operating cost. A store considering a complete move needs a separate email acceptance decision: Postscript now advertises Email, but its public pricing page links to an email waitlist. Confirm actual access and Shopify recovery functionality before scheduling an email cutover.</p>
<h2>What the documented integration actually shares</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564064-sync-postscript-coupons-to-klaviyo">Postscript’s coupon sync guide</a> supports using the same subscriber coupon in Klaviyo email and Postscript SMS. It requires an active integration and an email address to match profiles. The latest generated code syncs, but its expiration and discount details do not. Keep offer terms in the system that generated the code, and test whether a later code overwrites one referenced by an already queued email. Coupon sharing is not evidence that all suppression decisions sync.</p>
<h2>Distinguish cart intent from checkout intent</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/35510464880027">Klaviyo documents Added to Cart separately from Checkout Started</a>. The earlier flow should exclude later checkout and purchase activity. For an existing Klaviyo checkout flow, inspect its trigger metric and actual purchase filter before adding another cart entry point. Postscript’s <a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564066-create-a-klaviyo-welcome-series">Klaviyo welcome-series guide</a> describes a Checkout Started cancellation trigger and notes that an abandoned-cart link requires that trigger. A welcome signup and a checkout are different jobs; do not treat a successful form integration as proof of a working recovery link.</p>
<h2>Proposed acceptance exercise: the deferred text</h2>
<p>Create an identified, eligible shopper who receives Klaviyo email and then enters a Postscript recovery flow. Arrange for the text to wait, buy during that wait, and inspect both systems’ final send decisions. Repeat after an SMS opt-out and with email-only permission. Record checkout ID, customer identifiers, event arrival time, scheduled send time and actual cancellation. If quiet hours defer a text, demonstrate that purchase and permission are checked again before release. These are proposed tests, not reported vendor results.</p>
<p>Use a welcome signup followed immediately by checkout to expose overlapping offers. Assign a single discount owner and a shared contact budget before launch. A local frequency cap in one tool cannot prove that the other tool will remain silent. Where the integration cannot enforce the intended cross-channel spacing, simplify the flow or establish a verified exclusion rather than relying on matching delays.</p>
<h2>Price the actual SMS workload</h2>
<p><a href="https://postscript.io/pricing">Postscript’s public pricing</a> separates SMS/MMS rates, carrier fees and plan charges; Starter shows a $49 monthly minimum spend. It also advertises Email with a waitlist link. Compare the offered account terms, recipient countries, message segments and optional services with the cost of the same eligible audience in Klaviyo. A low per-message rate says little if copy creates extra segments or a minimum dominates a small recovery program. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<p>Build a monthly worksheet from eligible abandoned checkouts rather than the entire phone database. Include welcome and campaign traffic when allocating shared plan charges, and show the recovery-only marginal cost separately. Add staff time for reconciling two send logs, maintaining coupon terms and answering text replies. Do not count a migrated subscriber twice as new reach.</p>
<h2>Two hypothetical baskets, two incentive decisions</h2>
<p>For the original $759 basket at a 38% gross margin, fixed goods cost is $470.58 and full-price gross profit is $288.42. A 9% code reduces revenue to $690.69 and gross profit to $220.11, costing $68.31 per order. Recovering the same gross profit requires about 1.31 discounted orders for each full-price order, before any messaging expense.</p>
<p>For a $236 basket at 60% margin, fixed goods cost is $94.40 and gross profit is $141.60. An 8% discount yields $217.12 revenue and $122.72 gross profit, a loss of $18.88 per order. A useful text that resolves a shipping question may preserve more profit than a new code. These are arithmetic examples, not tested offers; payment fees, fulfillment, shipping subsidies, returns, tax and platform costs are excluded.</p>
<h2>Give the text an unresolved job</h2>
<p>For a financing or BNPL abandonment, link to accurate payment information without promising approval. For a missing gift message, explain how to add it and verify that the return link preserves the field. Let email carry detailed sizing, subscription and bundle terms; use SMS for a concise next action only when the shopper is eligible. A repeat buyer with saved details may need a working checkout link rather than another introductory pitch.</p>
<p>Test the destination on a mobile device after changing variants and quantity. For sold-out sizes, remove unavailable items from the promised action or route to a relevant alternative; never imply a code fixes inventory. A B2B basket below minimum order quantity needs quantity guidance, and international shipping shock needs an accurate landed-cost explanation. Check welcome-code stacking before issuing a second offer. These cases replace a fixed universal timing ladder.</p>
<h2>Move SMS without replaying old carts</h2>
<p>Export permission provenance and opt-outs alongside subscriber identifiers, then reconcile them in the receiving account. Inventory Klaviyo SMS flows, Shopify recovery and any other senders. Choose whether pending texts will finish with the old owner or be cancelled; keep a send ledger so the new owner does not replay already contacted carts. Move one flow after sample links, purchase exits and coupons pass. Leave email ownership explicit throughout.</p>
<p>If a cancellation or identity match fails, pause new SMS enrollment and reconcile pending messages before restoring the former owner. Retain old reporting exports and identify the cutover time so a changed attribution window is not mistaken for improved recovery. Compare order IDs across systems, then use a suitably sized holdout among eligible shoppers to estimate incremental gross profit after discounts and channel costs. Do not add both platforms’ attributed revenue together.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
